114
00:05:32,04 --> 00:05:33,04
You now have a better sense

115
00:05:33,04 --> 00:05:35,01
of how to manage the application state

116
00:05:35,01 --> 00:05:36,07
with the atomic approach.

117
00:05:36,07 --> 00:05:38,00
With the Recoil library,

118
00:05:38,00 --> 00:05:40,02
atoms are used as independent objects

119
00:05:40,02 --> 00:05:42,05
containing a single source of the state.

120
00:05:42,05 --> 00:05:43,06
It's like the state variables

121
00:05:43,06 --> 00:05:45,07
with useState with the React API,

122
00:05:45,07 --> 00:05:47,06
but with Recoil, the state variables

123
00:05:47,06 --> 00:05:50,04
are not attached to one component only.

124
00:05:50,04 --> 00:05:52,05
They act as independent pieces of state

125
00:05:52,05 --> 00:05:54,02
that can be shared between components

126
00:05:54,02 --> 00:05:56,07
and can be accessible everywhere,

127
00:05:56,07 --> 00:05:58,03
and the benefits are multiple.

128
00:05:58,03 --> 00:06:02,00
Less boilerplate code, a shorter and much simpler syntax,

129
00:06:02,00 --> 00:06:03,08
a better separation of concerns

130
00:06:03,08 --> 00:06:06,07
with a clean separation between where the state lives

131
00:06:06,07 --> 00:06:08,09
and where the state is consumed.

132
00:06:08,09 --> 00:06:10,02
And it is important to highlight

133
00:06:10,02 --> 00:06:12,08
that the learning curve is quick and easy.

134
00:06:12,08 --> 00:06:14,09
So this is one more reasons to get you excited

135
00:06:14,09 --> 00:06:15,00
and motivated about working with Recoil.

